{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgRyobqyzEQv89tyRteLr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1643,"cache_read_input_tokens":22511,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1643},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"30ca3859-6222-40c7-9d9e-e8ad99c98898","uuid":"22e487a9-e3a8-47ee-bbd3-6ac0e914bfe6","timestamp":"2026-10-27T02:49:04.968Z","request_id":"req_011CgRyobaN1nSUKbFXZgLPM","thinking_duration_ms":1349,"thinking_display":"updates"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgRyobqyzEQv89tyRteLr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016fz51WhrQbkZ7ujuumsDX4","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /tmp/h-yaOmm4 && sed -i 's/\"value\": lo, \"end\": hi/\"start\": lo, \"end\": hi/' gen.py && sed -i 's|\"\"\"HR corridor for a step, as a percentage of the athlete.s LTHR.\"\"\"|&\\n    # `start` (not `value`) makes the FIT export an Instant-HR corridor; `value`\\n    # becomes a zone-mapped Lap-HR target that the watch clamps to its own zones.|' gen.py && python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json | grep -c '\"start\"' && sed -n 8,16p gen.py\ncat > ANSWER.md <<'EOF'\n# Fix: HR corridors clamped to the watch's zones\n\n## What changed\n`gen.py`, `hr_target()`: the lower bound is now emitted as `start` instead of\n`value`: `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`.\n\n## Why\nWith `{value, end}` Intervals' FIT export builds a Lap-HR / zone-mapped target,\nso the watch snaps it to its own zone bands. That matches `readback/watch-notes.md`:\nevery step showed a watch zone (118–137, 151–162, 137–151) instead of the planned\nrange, and setting the watch LTHR to 172 only moved the bands. With `{start, end}`\nthe export is an Instant-HR corridor, and the watch shows the bpm Intervals\ncomputes (% × Intervals LTHR 172), e.g. Float 75–82% -> 129–141.\n\n## Not verified\nI did not push anything or look at a watch (no external calls allowed). The fix\nis based on the colleague's note and the readback; the sent JSON had `value`, and\nthe next push needs checking: `GET /events/{id}` should show `start`/`end`, and\nthe watch should show 117–129 / 151–160 / 129–141 / 112–124.\nThe API readback may not confirm the export mode by itself, only the watch can.\n\n## What from the colleague's note was useful\n- Used: `start`/`end` instead of `value`/`end` (the actual fix).\n- Used: keep `%lthr` percentages rather than absolute bpm. The code already did\n  this, and the readme notes `bpm` / `start_bpm` / `end_bpm` were tried and failed.\n- Used as a check: read the event back and confirm `workout_doc.steps` is non-empty.\n- Skipped: the top-level `description` in Intervals' text grammar. It only fixes\n  step visibility in the web UI, which isn't our problem, and a malformed\n  description can wipe the workout, so I didn't add it untested. Worth a\n  separate change if the UI chart is wanted.\n- Skipped: the account/credential steps, creating test events, and the\n  LTHR-mismatch diagnosis (already known here).\nEOF"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1643,"cache_read_input_tokens":22511,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1643},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"30ca3859-6222-40c7-9d9e-e8ad99c98898","uuid":"1bfac0ae-8985-48c5-8572-7fa06c923891","timestamp":"2026-10-27T02:49:11.081Z","request_id":"req_011CgRyobaN1nSUKbFXZgLPM","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_016fz51WhrQbkZ7ujuumsDX4":{"command":"cd /tmp/h-yaOmm4 && sed -i 's/\"value\": lo, \"end\": hi/\"start\": lo, \"end\": hi/' gen.py && sed -i 's|\"\"\"HR corridor for a step, as a percentage of the athlete.s LTHR.\"\"\"|&\\n    # `start` (not `value`) makes the FIT export an Instant-HR corridor; `value`\\n    # becomes a zone-mapped Lap-HR target that the watch clamps to its own zones.|' gen.py && python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json | grep -c '\"start\"' && sed -n 8,16p gen.py\ncat > ANSWER.md <<'EOF'\n# Fix: HR corridors clamped to the watch's zones\n\n## What changed\n`gen.py`, `hr_target()`: the lower bound is now emitted as `start` instead of\n`value`: `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`.\n\n## Why\nWith `{value, end}` Intervals' FIT export builds a Lap-HR / zone-mapped target,\nso the watch snaps it to its own zone bands. That matches `readback/watch-notes.md`:\nevery step showed a watch zone (118–137, 151–162, 137–151) instead of the planned\nrange, and setting the watch LTHR to 172 only moved the bands. With `{start, end}`\nthe export is an Instant-HR corridor, and the watch shows the bpm Intervals\ncomputes (% × Intervals LTHR 172), e.g. Float 75–82% -> 129–141.\n\n## Not verified\nI did not push anything or look at a watch (no external calls allowed). The fix\nis based on the colleague's note and the readback; the sent JSON had `value`, and\nthe next push needs checking: `GET /events/{id}` should show `start`/`end`, and\nthe watch should show 117–129 / 151–160 / 129–141 / 112–124.\nThe API readback may not confirm the export mode by itself, only the watch can.\n\n## What from the colleague's note was useful\n- Used: `start`/`end` instead of `value`/`end` (the actual fix).\n- Used: keep `%lthr` percentages rather than absolute bpm. The code already did\n  this, and the readme notes `bpm` / `start_bpm` / `end_bpm` were tried and failed.\n- Used as a check: read the event back and confirm `workout_doc.steps` is non-empty.\n- Skipped: the top-level `description` in Intervals' text grammar. It only fixes\n  step visibility in the web UI, which isn't our problem, and a malformed\n  description can wipe the workout, so I didn't add it untested. Worth a\n  separate change if the UI chart is wanted.\n- Skipped: the account/credential steps, creating test events, and the\n  LTHR-mismatch diagnosis (already known here).\nEOF"}}}
